rigctld: judge a read by its own errno

scanfc(), which reads every command and argument from the client, gave up
whenever errno was EINVAL, without having cleared it before fscanf() and
whether or not fscanf() had failed. An EINVAL left behind by any earlier
call in the client's thread therefore ended the connection at the next
command. bionic leaves one when the kernel cannot name a memory mapping
(prctl PR_SET_VMA): under qemu, where the arm64 build is tested, no client
got an answer.

diff --git a/tests/rigctl_parse.c b/tests/rigctl_parse.c
--- a/tests/rigctl_parse.c
+++ b/tests/rigctl_parse.c
@@ -569,7 +569,9 @@ static int scanfc(FILE *fin, const char *format, void *p)
         int ret;
         *(char *)p = 0;
 
-
+        /* [RADE] errno is looked at below, and says something about this
+         * fscanf() only if it is cleared first and fscanf() then fails. */
+        errno = 0;
         ret = fscanf(fin, format, p);
 
         if (ret < 0)
@@ -590,7 +592,7 @@ static int scanfc(FILE *fin, const char *format, void *p)
 
         if (ret < 1) { rig_debug(RIG_DEBUG_TRACE, "%s: ret=%d\n", __func__, ret); }
 
-        if (errno == 22) { return  -22; }
+        if (ret < 1 && errno == 22) { return  -22; }
 
         if (ferror(fin)) { rig_debug(RIG_DEBUG_ERR, "%s: errno=%d, %s\n", __func__, errno, strerror(errno)); }
 
